<ResourceDictionary xmlns="https://github.com/avaloniaui"
                    xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml">
  <!--
    The bridge between Fluent's own resource names and our palette.

    `DESIGN.md` says the colour system has two halves and the views are only
    one of them. `App.axaml`'s `ColorPaletteResources` is most of the second —
    two properties that repoint every accent and the window ground. This file
    is the rest: the keys Fluent does *not* derive from the accent, which it
    instead hard-codes as translucent black and white.

    **Why these need naming one at a time.** A ColorPaletteResources entry is a
    seed the theme derives a family from, so `Accent` fixes fifty controls. A
    key like `TextControlBackground` is a leaf — nothing else is computed from
    it, so nothing else changes when it does, and the only way to correct it is
    to say so. That is why this file is a list rather than two lines, and why
    it is a separate file rather than more of `Palette.axaml`: the palette is
    what the design *says*, this is what Fluent has to be *told*.

    Unlike `ColorPaletteResources`, these resolve normally, so they name roles.
    A literal here would be a defect the same as one in a view.
  -->

  <!-- ===== Fields ==================================================== -->
  <!--
    Text boxes, numeric fields and combo boxes, everywhere: dialogs, dockers,
    the tool options bar, flyouts, and anything added later. There is no list of
    call sites to keep in step because there is no call site — a control that
    has never heard of this file still comes out right, which is the whole
    reason to fix it here rather than with a style per control.

    **A field is a well, not a raised surface**, and this is a correction to the
    first answer rather than the first answer. Fluent's ground is `#66000000` —
    40% black, so a field darkened whatever it landed on by an unpredictable
    amount and *hovering deepened the hole*. The fix for that was a white tint,
    read off the reference, which lifted a field above its panel.

    Wrong direction, per the owner: a field takes the **darkest** surface, so it
    reads as a well cut into the panel with the panel's own edge around it. That
    is the "solidity" the alternate reference has and ours did not — a field on
    a docker, a field on a dialog and a field in a flyout are then the same
    colour, and the surface behind them is what changes.

    The tint is kept for the hover, because the *direction* argument still holds
    at the point of contact: pointing at something should make it lighter, never
    darker. It composites over the well rather than over the panel now.
  -->
  <StaticResource x:Key="TextControlBackground" ResourceKey="BackgroundPrimaryBrush" />
  <StaticResource x:Key="TextControlBackgroundPointerOver" ResourceKey="FieldGroundHoverBrush" />
  <StaticResource x:Key="TextControlBackgroundFocused" ResourceKey="BackgroundPrimaryBrush" />
  <StaticResource x:Key="TextControlBackgroundDisabled" ResourceKey="FieldGroundDisabledBrush" />

  <!--
    `#99ffffff` is 60% white, which on a dark panel is a bright wire around
    every field — the single loudest thing in a form, drawn around the part that
    is only a container. A border is a boundary, not an emphasis: it takes the
    strong border role, the same hairline a raised edge uses.
  -->
  <StaticResource x:Key="TextControlBorderBrush" ResourceKey="BorderStrongBrush" />
  <StaticResource x:Key="TextControlBorderBrushPointerOver" ResourceKey="BorderStrongBrush" />
  <StaticResource x:Key="TextControlPlaceholderForeground" ResourceKey="TextSecondaryBrush" />

  <!--
    Fluent pins every field's font to this resource (default 14), and a style
    setter beats inheritance — so the `:is(TopLevel)` FontSize=12 reached every
    label and *no* field. At 14, a line box no longer fits a 22px field and the
    digits lost their bottoms: "100" read as "1ᴼᴼ". The density scale's base
    font and this key must be the same number.
  -->
  <x:Double x:Key="ControlContentThemeFontSize">12</x:Double>

  <!--
    Fluent's slider template reserves 15px ABOVE and BELOW the track for tick
    bars, as fixed grid rows — so inside our 24px slider the track assembly
    sat 15px down and read four pixels low against the label beside it. The
    earlier VerticalAlignment fixes could not win against fixed row heights;
    this can, because the rows read their height from these keys. Nothing in
    this application shows slider ticks.
  -->
  <GridLength x:Key="SliderPreContentMargin">*</GridLength>
  <GridLength x:Key="SliderPostContentMargin">*</GridLength>

  <!--
    `TextControlBorderBrushFocused` is deliberately absent: Fluent derives it
    from the accent, so it is already our violet. Restating it here would be a
    second place to change it and a first place for the two to disagree.
  -->

  <StaticResource x:Key="ComboBoxBackground" ResourceKey="BackgroundPrimaryBrush" />
  <StaticResource x:Key="ComboBoxBackgroundPointerOver" ResourceKey="FieldGroundHoverBrush" />
  <StaticResource x:Key="ComboBoxBackgroundPressed" ResourceKey="FieldGroundHoverBrush" />

  <!--
    Every field the same shape as every other boxed thing on the page.

    The combo was rounded and the numeric field square, side by side in the same
    row of the Layers docker — reported as "every boxed element has rounded
    corners except those text boxes", and visible the moment the two are cropped
    together. It is one value rather than four because a corner radius is the
    kind of number that only reads as deliberate when nothing disagrees with it.
  -->
  <CornerRadius x:Key="FieldCornerRadius">4</CornerRadius>
  <StaticResource x:Key="ComboBoxBorderBrush" ResourceKey="BorderStrongBrush" />

  <!-- ===== Buttons =================================================== -->
  <!--
    A button is the bar's own dark surface with a rim light along its top —
    read off the reference, where the toolbar's buttons are the same dark as
    the toolbar and only the lit top edge says "pressable". Fluent's default
    is `#33ffffff`, 20% white, which made every unranked button a pale box and
    the toolbar a wall of them.

    The rim is the BorderBrush: a vertical white-to-nothing gradient that a
    1px border samples by position, so the top edge catches light, the sides
    taper and the bottom gets none. One brush, no second element.
  -->
  <StaticResource x:Key="ButtonBackground" ResourceKey="SurfacePanelBrush" />
  <StaticResource x:Key="ButtonBackgroundPointerOver" ResourceKey="SurfaceElevatedBrush" />
  <StaticResource x:Key="ButtonBackgroundPressed" ResourceKey="BackgroundPrimaryBrush" />
  <StaticResource x:Key="ButtonBorderBrush" ResourceKey="RimLightBrush" />
  <StaticResource x:Key="ButtonBorderBrushPointerOver" ResourceKey="RimLightBrush" />
  <StaticResource x:Key="ToggleButtonBackground" ResourceKey="SurfacePanelBrush" />
  <StaticResource x:Key="ToggleButtonBackgroundPointerOver" ResourceKey="SurfaceElevatedBrush" />
  <StaticResource x:Key="ToggleButtonBorderBrush" ResourceKey="RimLightBrush" />

  <!-- ===== Things that float ========================================= -->
  <!--
    Context menus, combo drop-downs and flyouts all sat on `#ff2b2b2b` — a flat
    neutral grey with no blue in it at all, which is why a right-click used to
    look like it belonged to a different application. It did: that value is
    Fluent's, and nothing in the palette had ever reached it.

    They take the elevated surface for the same reason dialogs do. Anything
    floating above the window goes one step up, which is the whole meaning of
    the four surfaces.
  -->
  <StaticResource x:Key="ComboBoxDropDownBackground" ResourceKey="SurfaceElevatedBrush" />
  <StaticResource x:Key="FlyoutPresenterBackground" ResourceKey="SurfaceElevatedBrush" />
  <StaticResource x:Key="MenuFlyoutPresenterBackground" ResourceKey="SurfaceElevatedBrush" />
</ResourceDictionary>
